-- title:
--   Connectivity of the configuration graph over a preconnected open set
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space and let $\Gamma$ be a configuration on $E$, assigning to each point $z$ a double cone $\Gamma(z)$ with apex angle $\vartheta_z$. Assume:
--
--   1. $\Gamma$ takes only finitely many values, i.e. its range is a finite set (finitely many cone types occur);
--   2. $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$;
--   3. every apex angle is at least $\vartheta$: $\vartheta \le \vartheta_z$ for all $z \in E$.
--
--   Then for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and every subset $U \subseteq E$ such that
--
--   * the number of cone types realized on $U$ is at most $n$, i.e. $\#\,\Gamma(U) \le n$,
--   * $U$ is open,
--   * $U$ is preconnected,
--
--   any two points $x, y \in U$ are connected in $G[U]$ — the graph on $E$ whose directed edges run from a vertex $z$ of $U$ to each point of the shifted cone $V^\Gamma[z]$, connectivity being taken in the underlying undirected graph, so that vertices outside $U$ may be used as intermediate points:
--   $$\forall x, y \in U, \qquad \mathrm{Conn}(\Gamma, U, x, y).$$
--
--   This is the connectivity theorem of Section 4 (Theorem 4.1), the continuous prelude on which the whole discrete construction rests: it says that the cone structure of a configuration with boundedly many types already links up any preconnected open region, so that later arguments only have to control the *quality* of the connecting paths (their length, their edge lengths, and their multiplicity) rather than their existence.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The natural number $n$ is an explicit induction parameter, universally quantified inside the conclusion: the statement is proved by induction on a bound for the number of cone types realized in $U$, and applying it to a given $U$ means instantiating $n$ with (a bound for) $\#\,\Gamma(U)$, which is legitimate because the range of $\Gamma$ is assumed finite. Note that "preconnected" is Mathlib's variant of connectedness that does not require nonemptiness, and that $\mathrm{Conn}$ refers to connectivity in the undirected graph underlying the directed graph $G[U]$.
